# 1.1 Variable Scoping & Immutability Patterns

## Start here: what problem are we solving?

Before learning React, you need to understand a simple JavaScript idea:

> A variable stores a value, and a variable can be created in different ways.

React also relies heavily on another idea:

> When application data changes, create a new value instead of secretly changing the old value.

These two ideas are connected. They make React code easier to reason about, test, and update.

---

## 1. `let`, `const`, and `var`

### `let`

Use `let` when the variable itself needs to be assigned a new value later.

```javascript
let score = 10;
score = 20;

console.log(score); // 20
```

### `const`

Use `const` when the variable binding should not be reassigned.

```javascript
const name = "Anu";

// name = "Ravi"; // ❌ TypeError
```

The word `const` does **not** mean that every nested value is frozen.

```javascript
const user = {
  name: "Anu",
  skills: ["JavaScript"]
};

user.name = "Ravi";          // ✅ the object can still be changed in JS
user.skills.push("React");   // ✅ the array can still be changed
```

For React state, however, treat objects and arrays as read-only and replace them with copies when updating them.

### `var`

`var` is older JavaScript syntax and has function-scoping rules that can surprise beginners. Modern React code normally uses `let` and `const`.

```javascript
if (true) {
  let inside = "block";
  var legacy = "also visible outside";
}

// console.log(inside); // ❌ block scoped
console.log(legacy);   // older var behavior
```

### Simple rule

```text
Use const by default.
Use let when reassignment is genuinely required.
Avoid var in new React code.
```

---

## 2. What does "scope" mean?

Scope answers:

> "Where can I use this variable?"

A block is usually the code inside `{ ... }` for an `if`, loop, or function block.

```javascript
{
  const city = "Chennai";
  console.log(city); // ✅
}

// console.log(city); // ❌ outside its block
```

Functions also create scope:

```javascript
function greet() {
  const message = "Hello";
  console.log(message);
}

greet();
// console.log(message); // ❌ message exists only inside greet
```

Understanding scope prevents accidental use of the wrong variable.

---

## 3. What is a reference?

Primitive values such as numbers and strings behave like independent values:

```javascript
let a = 10;
let b = a;

b = 20;

console.log(a); // 10
console.log(b); // 20
```

Objects are different. A variable stores a reference to the object.

```javascript
const user1 = { name: "Meera" };
const user2 = user1;

user2.name = "Priya";

console.log(user1.name); // "Priya"
```

Both variables refer to the same object.

This is one of the reasons direct mutation can produce confusing React bugs.

---

## 4. Mutation vs replacement

### Mutation

Mutation changes the existing object:

```javascript
user.name = "Priya";
```

### Replacement

Replacement creates another object:

```javascript
const updatedUser = {
  ...user,
  name: "Priya"
};
```

Think of it like this:

```text
Mutation:
old object ──changed in place──> same object

Replacement:
old object ────────────────┐
                           ├──> new object
                           └──> old object remains unchanged
```

---

## 5. Why React prefers immutable updates

React state should be treated as read-only. When an object in state needs to change, make a new object and pass it to the state setter.

```jsx
const [profile, setProfile] = useState({
  name: "Anu",
  level: 1
});

function levelUp() {
  setProfile(previous => ({
    ...previous,
    level: previous.level + 1
  }));
}
```

The previous state is used to construct the next state.

This pattern is especially important for nested objects.

```javascript
const [student, setStudent] = useState({
  name: "Anu",
  address: {
    city: "Coimbatore"
  }
});

setStudent(previous => ({
  ...previous,
  address: {
    ...previous.address,
    city: "Chennai"
  }
}));
```

Notice that we copied every level that we update.

---

## 6. Arrays in React state

Do not use mutating methods such as `push()` or `pop()` directly on state arrays.

### ❌ Mutation

```javascript
items.push(newItem);
setItems(items);
```

### ✅ Replacement

```javascript
setItems(previous => [...previous, newItem]);
```

Remove an item with `filter()`:

```javascript
setItems(previous =>
  previous.filter(item => item.id !== idToRemove)
);
```

Update one item with `map()`:

```javascript
setItems(previous =>
  previous.map(item =>
    item.id === id
      ? { ...item, completed: true }
      : item
  )
);
```

---

## 7. Spread syntax: why does `...` appear?

The spread operator copies enumerable properties from an object.

```javascript
const user = {
  name: "Anu",
  role: "Student"
};

const copy = {
  ...user
};

console.log(copy);
```

You can override a property by writing it after the spread:

```javascript
const promotedUser = {
  ...user,
  role: "Mentor"
};
```

Later properties win when keys collide.

```javascript
const result = {
  role: "Student",
  ...user
};

console.log(result.role); // "Student" if user.role is "Student"
```

The ordering matters.

---

## 8. A tiny React example from scratch

```jsx
import { useState } from "react";

export default function App() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState({
    name: "Anu",
    level: 1
  });

  const promote = () => {
    setUser(previous => ({
      ...previous,
      level: previous.level + 1
    }));
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{user.name}</h1>
      <p>Level: {user.level}</p>
      <button onClick={promote}>Promote</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Read it slowly

1. `useState(...)` gives React something to remember.
2. `user` is the current value.
3. `setUser` requests a new value.
4. `previous => ({ ...previous, ... })` copies the old object.
5. `level` gets a new value.
6. React renders the component again using the new state.

---

## 9. Common beginner mistakes

### Mistake 1: changing state directly

```javascript
profile.level += 1; // ❌
```

Use:

```javascript
setProfile(previous => ({
  ...previous,
  level: previous.level + 1
}));
```

### Mistake 2: forgetting the previous fields

```javascript
setProfile({
  level: 2
});
```

This replaces the whole object and loses `name` and `role`.

Use:

```javascript
setProfile(previous => ({
  ...previous,
  level: 2
}));
```

### Mistake 3: confusing `const` with deep immutability

`const` prevents reassignment of the variable binding. It does not freeze an object.

React's immutability rule is a programming discipline: treat state values as read-only and replace them when updating.

---

## Mini practice

Predict the output:

```javascript
const original = { name: "A" };
const copy = { ...original, name: "B" };

console.log(original.name);
console.log(copy.name);
```

Answer:

```text
A
B
```

Now predict:

```javascript
const original = { name: "A" };
const copy = original;
copy.name = "B";

console.log(original.name);
```

Answer:

```text
B
```

That difference is the heart of reference vs copy behavior.

## Recap

You should now understand:

- `const` vs `let` vs `var`
- block and function scope
- primitive values vs object references
- mutation vs replacement
- object spread
- immutable object updates
- immutable array updates
- why these patterns matter in React

Further reading:
- React: Updating Objects in State — https://react.dev/learn/updating-objects-in-state
- React: Updating Arrays in State — https://react.dev/learn/updating-arrays-in-state
- W3Schools: React tutorial — https://www.w3schools.com/react/
- GeeksforGeeks: Getting Started with React — https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/reactjs/getting-started-with-react/

